# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A category that holds both transactions and other categories
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule A category with its own transactions shows them on an Other row
Description A category that contains other categories can still be chosen for a
Description transaction. Expenses:Auto has Fuel and Insurance under it and also
Description has petrol bought before anyone made a Fuel category, and cash spent
Description on the car that belongs nowhere more precise.
Description
Description The transaction is associated with the containing category, exactly as
Description chosen. The report is where it shows differently: the containing
Description category carries the total of everything beneath it, and its own
Description transactions appear as a row under it called Other, so that the rows
Description underneath add up to the row above.
Details Without that row the arithmetic of the report is wrong in a way that is
Details hard to see: Auto reads 100,948.56, its children add to 99,862.97, and
Details the missing 1,085.59 is money posted to Auto itself with nothing on the
Details page to say so. A reader checking the column would find the error and
Details have no way to explain it.
Details
Details The row is not a category. Nobody chooses it, nothing is posted to it,
Details and it is not in the chart of accounts. It exists for the length of the
Details report, which is why choosing the containing category is the whole of
Details what a person does.
Details
Details The name follows Quicken, because the reports are checked against
Details Quicken's: the word Other, then the containing category's path with the
Details section name taken off. So Expenses:Auto gives "Other Auto", and
Details Expenses:Cash Expenditures:Foreign Travel gives "Other Cash
Details Expenditures:Foreign Travel".
Examples: OtherRowNaming
| ContainingCategory                         | TheRowIsCalled                         |
| Expenses:Auto                              | Other Auto                             |
| Expenses:Cash Expenditures                 | Other Cash Expenditures                |
| Expenses:Cash Expenditures:Foreign Travel  | Other Cash Expenditures:Foreign Travel |
| Income:Div Income                          | Other Div Income                       |
| Income:Tax Refund                          | Other Tax Refund                       |

Attributes OtherRowNaming
| Attribute          | Type        | Default |
| ContainingCategory | AccountPath |         |
| TheRowIsCalled     | Text        |         |

Scenario A category with its own transactions and children shows an Other row
Description Forty on petrol under Fuel, ten on something to do with the car
Description posted to Auto itself. Auto totals fifty, and the two rows beneath it
Description add to fifty.
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                   | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking        | Bank    | false       |
| Expenses:Auto          | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel     | Expense | false       |
Given dated postings are : DatedPostingLine
| Date       | Account            | Amount |
| 2026-03-01 | Assets:Checking    | -40.00 |
| 2026-03-01 | Expenses:Auto:Fuel | 40.00  |
| 2026-03-02 | Assets:Checking    | -10.00 |
| 2026-03-02 | Expenses:Auto      | 10.00  |
When report run : ReportSpec Vertical
| GroupBy | Category |
| From | 2026-01-01 |
| To   | 2026-12-31 |
Then report rows are : ReportRow
| Account            | Amount | Level | IsSubtotal |
| Expenses:Auto      | 50.00  | 1     | true       |
| Expenses:Auto:Fuel | 40.00  | 2     | false      |
| Other Auto         | 10.00  | 2     | false      |
# Auto is the total of everything in it. Beneath it, Fuel's forty and its own
# ten, which add to the fifty above. The Other row comes last, after the real
# categories, which is where Quicken puts it -- and these reports are checked
# against Quicken's.

